00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.590 align:start position:0%
 
hey everyone this is kevin from  thechesswebsite.com and today we're  going to be going over my top nine  aggressive chess defenses that i like to  use i know many of you are aggressive  players like i am and so today we're  going to be looking at the openings that  i use as black if i want to be extremely  aggressive i'll be curious in the  comments below what your favorite  aggressive chess defense is and see if  it lines up with what i have in my list  so without further ado let's go ahead  and hop into my top nine list  coming in at number nine we have the  dragon variation in the sicilian defense  the sicilian being one of the sharpest  lines in all of chess for black after e4  c5 here and then knight to f3  pawn to d6 you usually see an exchange  in the center of the board here then  after knight to f6 attacking the pawn  knight to c3 defending that pawn black  plays pawn to g6 and this opening is  chaotic it doesn't matter if white plays  the main line to bishop to e3 looking  for a quick development to castle on the  queen side or if they play the hyper  aggressive pond f4 the leaven fish  attack either way  it's going to be madness but white  usually looks to castle on the queen  side black castle on the king side and  it just becomes a race of throwing  everything you have at your opponent's  king  and it gets wild there's a lot of other  variations you can look at the  accelerated drag and the  hyper-accelerated dragon  those are just some shortcuts but they  are not as aggressive as the dragon  variation so if you're looking for  something extremely aggressive in the  sicilian i definitely recommend the  dragon coming in at number eight we have  the faulkner counter gambit which is a  direct attack on the king's gambit  e4 e5 and then pawn to f4 this is the  king's game it is my favorite line as  white extremely aggressive from white  and black says i can play just as  aggressive pawn here to d5 pushing both  of the central pawns  opening up for this light square bishop  to get involved also preparing to take  here on e4 which would attack this f3  square which normally this knight is  coming here to f3  this is an extremely aggressive line for  black these games typically don't last  more than 30 moves because there's so  much chaos there's so many traps that  both sides can fall into so this is  definitely something if you are playing  as white you better be prepared because  black is ready for a fight so that's why  it is number eight on our list  coming in at number seven is the nemzo  indian defense after pawn two d4 then f6  white really tries to control the center  with the queen side pawns here after e6  if white plays knight to c3 then black  responds with bishop here 2b4 pinning  down this knight and many times there's  an exchange with the bishop in the  knight white's left with double pawns  here on the c file many players as white  will try to whatever they can to avoid  these lines because there's so many  aggressive lines for black and also it  scores favorably from black's  perspective so you may even see instead  of knight to c3 things like knight to f3  anything they can do to make sure that  they do not allow their opponent to play  the nemzo indian defense  coming in at number six we have the  icelandic game it already starts from a  pretty aggressive line from black and  that is the scandinavian offense with e4  d5 but after the pawn takes black avoids  the most common line if queen takes here  knight to c3 kicking the queen back and  black says we're going to go ahead and  give you that material let's play knight  to f6 you can defend it with pawn here  to c4 but then we're going to play pawn  e6 opening up for both of our bishops  now get involved into the game black has  this  queen here on d8 semi-open file that he  can start getting involved into the game  the knight on f6 has already developed  and black has a lot of room to attack  the opponent if you have not tried this  one he's definitely gonna want to check  out the video i did on the icelandic  gambit it is a fun one for sure  i am curious if you guys like this video  if you wouldn't mind hitting the like  button that definitely helps out the  channel a lot i think a lot of people  are going to like this video and i am  just curious to see what other people  think are their most aggressive chess  defenses that they they like to play if  you wouldn't mind let me know in the  comments below  what your favorite chess defense is  coming in at number five we have the  schliemann gambit which derives from the  rye lopez fairly common opening in chess  e4 e5  knight to f3 knight to c6 and after  bishop to b5 many times white is in the  driver's seat the slimy game it says not  so fast my friend we are going to play  pawn to f5  we're gonna give up material here on f5  to control the center of the board  have no fear if you look and say the  king side is not well protected black  usually ends up castling on the queen  side and this is pure chaos you also  have a lot of opportunity to play this  defense because many times white really  likes to play the right lopez they feel  like it's safe they feel like they're in  a good spot and then you hit them with  the slim and gamut and they just have no  idea what to do  coming in at number four is one of my  favorite defenses and that is the  latvian gambit people just do not expect  you to play it and that is e4  e5  knight f3 and then pawn two f5  immediately striking the center of the  board here i think this may just come  from the fact that i like to play a lot  of kings gambit so i'm just extremely  comfortable always pushing my king side  pawn here on the f file up to control  the center of the board  get a lot of other material involved and  try to put a lot of pressure on my  opponent but the latvian gambit is a fun  one pure chaos if you haven't tried it  definitely check out the video i did on  that one it is a ton of fun but it is  chaos  coming in at number three we have the  russo game but one of the most common  openings in chess is the italian game  with e45 and that f3 knight c6 and then  bishop here to c4  and black says okay you're trying to be  pretty aggressive here we are going to  be just as aggressive and that is  playing pawn to  f5 putting a lot of pressure on the  central square here and a lot of players  do not know how to respond so black many  times can have the upper hand here so if  you haven't checked this one out i  definitely recommend it is an extremely  aggressive line from black  coming in at number two we have the  staffer gambit fan favorite and that  derives from the pet drive defense after  e4 e5 knight f3 knight to f6 if you  watched much chess on youtube you've  probably seen a lot of videos on the  stafford gambit and it is a ton of fun  to play after the knight takes here on  e5 the main line is d6 forcing the  knight to come back here to f3  but that's not as fun as the stafford  gambit and that is knight to  c6 and then after the knight takes then  black plays pawn takes c6 you can see  everything is open for black  both of the bishops are ready to get  into action the queen here is ready to  get into the action and black already  has a developed piece here on f6  extremely aggressive line from black and  a ton of fun to play  curious in the comments below if the  stafford gambit ranks extremely high on  your list of defenses to play as black  because i  personally love this opening  coming in at number one we have the  traxler counter attack starts out with  e4  e5 and then f3 and knight c6 bishop here  to c4 the italian game knights f6 and  then white plays the fried liver attack  you've probably come across this or  maybe even played this yourself with  knight to  g5 and then you have the traxler counter  attack i really like playing this in  person as well because then you can play  and look at your opponent let them know  that you're the captain now you're in  the driver's seat you don't care if they  play the fried liver attack you have  just as strong attack as they do and  that is bishop here to c5 so yeah you  can attack my f7 square i don't care  because the pain is coming i'm attacking  your f2 square and we can just duke it  out till the end of the game  extremely aggressive line from black and  that is why it is number one on my list  thank you guys so much for watching the  video hopefully you enjoyed my top nine  aggressive chess defenses if there was a  defense that i left out  please let me know in the comments below  also curious if you agree with my  assessment on what i think the best ones  are to play  thank you guys so much for watching the  video and i will see you guys next time

